comments.text:
Demand-cone deletion spike disproved simple Phase-B removal. Phase-A-only retained end-to-end Enum behavior and the repeated schedule control, but lost the nested x < 4 predicate's direct surface/resolution. Of 38 settled callable-flow resolution occurrences, 5 were absent from every exact CallTargetSummary globally: split_positive (2), split_with, split_while, and drop_while lambdas. These are first-class flows whose producer and observed public surface do not meet until demand propagation. They require first refinement, not target preservation.

Therefore:
- close exact CallTargetSummary targets directly;
- carry exact target correlations backward where they exist;
- carry unresolved/public surfaces separately;
- when an actual surface reaches its LocalCallableProducer, refine producer + surface into a target;
- monotonically expand and re-settle the demand cone from those actual flow resolutions;
- delete type_derived_flow_resolutions, which predicts targets from producer value Ty before demand and is incomplete;
- delete ground_surface_for_template_in_session, which scans global sibling executables to select a fitting instantiation. A template may refine only against grounded evidence carried by the same callable flow; otherwise it remains unresolved.

A disposable monotone-expansion spike is the current gate.
